var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"constant_charge_collocation/#Overview","page":"Step 1: Collocation","text":"The simplest version of the BEM electrostatics solver in this package is a point collocation solver with straight line constant charge density basis elements. This is a comparison point to benchmark the \"higher-order\" methods that we will be implementing and mainly relying on.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"constant_charge_collocation/#Planar-kernel","page":"Step 1: Collocation","text":"Renau, Read, and Brunt [1] analytically integrate (and approximate the result where necessary) to obtain a formula that directly gives their matrix elements. By contrast, we will take the path of singular quadrature. It might be a bit slower, but it will converge to the exact value (better than the approximate one that RRH give), and singular quadrature is the main challenge of higher-order methods anyways. The planar kernel is an especially nice test case, because of its simple form, with an explicit weak (logarithmic) divergence. In 2D BEM electrostatics, logarithmic divergences are going to be the main/only form of divergence that we see (at least until we get to hypersingular kernels for Calderon preconditioning), but it already get's more complicated with axisymmetric kernels and the elliptic integrals that come into play.","title":"Planar kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"constant_charge_collocation/#Quadrature-method","page":"Step 1: Collocation","text":"We will see logarithmic divergences for the diagonal matrix elements (the \"self-interaction\") and for matrix elements that correspond to adjacent elements, because one element's edge will \"touch\" the the edge of its neighbor. We need in general to handle those two cases separately, so there needs to be some awareness of what the mesh is like to dispatch those matrix elements to the correct integration function. We will have multiple logarithmic-weighted quadrature routines to benchmark against each other.\n\nlog-weighted Gaussian quadrature\nlog-weighted Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature\ntanh-sinh quadrature","title":"Quadrature method"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"using ElectrostaticOptics\nusing StaticArrays\nusing Plots","title":"AbstractCurve2D"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Using-AbstractCurve2D","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"","title":"Using AbstractCurve2D"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Line-segment","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"Easy, just give start and end points.\n\np₁ = SVector(0.0, 2.0)\np₂ = SVector(1.0, 1.0)\n\nline = ElectrostaticOptics.LineSeg(p₁, p₂)\nplot(line, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Line segment"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#CircArc","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"Circular arc segment.  Give center, radius, and start/end angles\n\no = SVector(1.0, 2.0)\nr = 1.0\nθ₁ = 0.5 * π\nθ₂ = 0.2 * π\n\narc = CircArc(o, r, θ₁, θ₂)\n\nplot(arc, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nLet's check the measure, should be 2pi r. measure(arc, 0.0), r * abs(θ₂ - θ₁)","title":"CircArc"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#EllipticArc","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"Stretch and shear a circular arc, then translate it. The result stores the transformed center and a matrix mapping the unit circle to the ellipse.\n\ncircle_arc = CircArc(SVector(0.0, 0.0), 1.0, 0.0, 1.5π)\nA = @SMatrix [2.0 0.5; 0.0 1.0]\noffset = SVector(3.0, 1.0)\nellipse_arc = affine_transform(circle_arc, A, offset)\n\nplot(circle_arc, label = \"Circle\", aspect_ratio = :equal)\nplot!(ellipse_arc, label = \"Affine image\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"EllipticArc"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#FuncCurve","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"You can define any function you like, e.g. a hyperbola.\n\nf(t) = SVector(cosh(t), sinh(t))\nhyperbola = FuncCurve(f, -1.0, 1.0)\n\nplot(hyperbola, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"FuncCurve"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Test-Filleting-(solve_fillet)","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"","title":"Test Filleting (solve_fillet)"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Straight-line-filleting","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"For straight lines, the fillet solve is trivial and should converge in one iteration (as long as the fillet radius is shorter than both lines).\n\np_corner = SVector(0.0, 0.0)\n\nlineA = LineSeg(SVector(1.0, -1.0), p_corner)\nlineB = LineSeg(p_corner, SVector(1.0, 1.0))\n\nplot(lineA, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nplot!(lineB)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nThe below gets summarized into solve_fillet_arc()\n\nR = 0.25\ntrims, center = solve_fillet(R, (lineA, lineB))\n\ntrim_pt_A = lineA(trims[1])\ntrim_pt_B = lineB(trims[2])\n\nvec_A = trim_pt_A - center\nvec_B = trim_pt_B - center\n\nθ₁ = mod(atan(vec_A[2], vec_A[1]), 2π)\nθ₂ = mod(atan(vec_B[2], vec_B[1]), 2π)\n\nfillet_arc = CircArc(center, R, θ₁, θ₂)\n\nplot!(fillet_arc, linecolor = :blue)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}","title":"Straight-line filleting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Fillet-between-two-arcs","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"arcA = CircArc(SVector(-1.0, 0.0), 1.0, -π / 2, 0.0)\narcB = CircArc(SVector(0.0, 1.0), 1.0, -π / 2, 0.0)\n\nplot(arcA, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nplot!(arcB)\n\nR = 0.25\ntrims, fillet_arc = solve_fillet_arc(R, (arcA, arcB))\nplot!(fillet_arc, linecolor = :blue)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}","title":"Fillet between two arcs"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Filleting-a-cusp","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"arcA = CircArc(SVector(-1.0, 0.0), 1.0, -π / 2, 0.0)\narcB = CircArc(SVector(1.0, 0.0), 1.0, 1.0 * π, 3 * π / 2)\n\nplot(arcA, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nplot!(arcB)\n\nR = 0.2\ntrims, fillet_arc = solve_fillet_arc(R, (arcA, arcB))\nplot!(fillet_arc, linecolor = :blue)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}","title":"Filleting a cusp"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/abstract_curve/#Weird-one","page":"AbstractCurve2D","text":"arcA = CircArc(SVector(-1.0, 0.0), 1.0, -π / 6, 0.0)\narcB = CircArc(SVector(-sqrt(3) / 2, -0.5), 1.0, π / 6, 7π / 6)\n\nplot(arcA, aspect_ratio = :equal)\nplot!(arcB)\n\nR = 0.5\ntrims, fillet_arc = solve_fillet_arc(R, (arcA, arcB))\nplot!(fillet_arc, linecolor = :blue)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Weird one"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract_mesh/#Overview","page":"Mesh","text":"The \"mesh\" is not really the \"mesh\" in the sense of a 3D model. It's not so important to know which geometry is where, but rather to be able to quickly know if two patches (that actually contain geometrical information) are connected with each other. That allows us to dispatch quadrature for adjacent elements to a numerical integration routine that is equipped to handle the edge divergence that can ocurr between these two elements. It's basically a list of patches, information about their connectivity, and a quick way to access the geometrical information during matrix assembly.\n\nThe basic idea for how to organize the geometry and topology information.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract_mesh/#Storing-the-actual-elements","page":"Mesh","text":"Overall: abstract type AbstractElement{N<:Integer,D<:Integer,T<:Real} end Here, N is the dimension of the space (so 2D or 3D space), D is the dimension of the boundary (I think it should alost always be N-1), and T is just the type of real number to use.\n\nIt should support a few basic geometry functions: (e::AbstractElement)(\\xi::T)::SVector{N,T} where {N,T} xiin 01 is the parameter representing the coordinate in the \"unit cell\" that gets mapped to real geometry. TODO is to figure out a nice way to allow multiple xi, e.g. in 3D.\n\njacobian(e::AbstractElement, \\xi::T)::SVector{N,D,T} where {N,D,T} This is the Jacobian for the mapping from the unit cell to the actual geometry\n\nThe rest of the functions don't even need individual implementations for each AbstractElement: they can be derived from the Jacobbian.  Maybe nice multiple dispatch to take J directly as input (to avoid re-calculating J multiple times) gram(e::AbstractElement, \\xi::T)::SVector{N,N,T} where {N,T} Gives the gram matrix J' * J.\n\nmeasure(e::AbstractElement, \\xi::T)::SVector{N,N,T} where {N,T} The measure for integration.  Gives sqrt(gram(e,\\xi))\n\nsurface_grad(e::AbstractElement, \\xi::T)::SVector{N,T} where {N,T} Surface gradient, for Maue regularized hypersingular kernel later on.\n\nnormal(e::AbstractElement, \\xi::T)::SVector{N,T} where {N,T} Normal vector.  For N=2 and D=1, (J[2], -J[1]) / norm(J)","title":"Storing the actual elements"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract_mesh/#Storing-the-topology","page":"Mesh","text":"Graphs.jl is probably nice for storing the topology.  It makes sense to have a directed graph without weights: we care about the direction of the elements for the hypersingular kernel.  SimpleDiGraph seems like the right option. We should make a Mesh struct that keeps the vector of AbstractEelemnts together with the topology graph.","title":"Storing the topology"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract_mesh/#Mappings-for-substitutions-during-quadrature","page":"Mesh","text":"Even with singularity subtraction, mappings like Telles and Duffy are nice to have to speed up quadrature convergence. The AbstractElement itself is basically already a mathematical mapping...  Might make sense to have an even more general AbstractMapping supertype that AbstractElement is derived from.","title":"Mappings for substitutions during quadrature"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract_mesh/#Generating-test-geometries","page":"Mesh","text":"A lightweight turtle implementation would be cute/fun.","title":"Generating test geometries"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"A. Renau, F. H. Read and J. N. Brunt. The charge-density method of solving electrostatic problems with and without the inclusion of space-charge. Journal of Physics E: Scientific Instruments 15, 347 (1982).\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"point_collocation/#General-Overview","page":"Collocation","text":"For a \"planar kernel\", we are assuming that the system extends along the z axis a very long distance compared to the length scale of our system's cross-section in the x-y plane. We can't actually make it infinite, because then the electrostatic energy diverges, so we just take the limit. The derivation follows [1], but our approach is a little bit different. They start out by integrating over the full source element to find the aggregate contribution from it at a specific test (collocation) point. This works for a collocation-specific code, but it doesn't generalize as nicely when we want to be able to take the same kernel and take a Galerkin BEM approach. We don't want to hard-program the integral, just the point kernel.","title":"General Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"point_collocation/#Derivation","page":"Collocation","text":"We start with the classic derivation for the potential from an infinite line of charge. Using Gauss's theorem, we know that the electric field flux leaving a cylindrical surface of height h and radius r, centered along the line charge with charge density lambda is\n\n2pi r h E=fraclambda hepsilon_0 \n\nThe electric field strength at distance r from the line charge is therefore\n\nE=fraclambda2pi repsilon_0 \n\nand the field points radially. The potential is found by integrating inward from a reference radius r_0, that defines the zero potential.\n\nbeginsplit\nphi(r)=intvecFcdot dvecx\n=int_r_0^r-fraclambda2piepsilon_0 RdR\n=leftfraclambda2piepsilon_0logleft(frac1rright)right_r_0^r\n=fraclambda2piepsilon_0logleft(fracr_0rright)\n=2lambda k_elogleft(fracr_0rright)\nendsplit\n\nAs you can see, taking r_0toinfty causes the potential to diverge, so we have no choice but to keep this finite reference radius hanging around. The point kernel should give the potential contribution per unit charge, so the formula that we actually program is (K for kernel)\n\nK(r)=2k_elogleft(fracr_0rright)\n\nIn principle, we could drop the constants out, but it's nice to know what the matrix elements of the BEM matrix physically will be.","title":"Derivation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/mesh/#Building-a-mesh","page":"Mesh","text":"using ElectrostaticOptics\nusing StaticArrays\nusing Plots","title":"Building a mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/mesh/#two-circles","page":"Mesh","text":"circle_mesh_A = circle(SVector(0.0, 2.0), 1.0)\ncircle_mesh_B = circle(SVector(5.0, 2.0), 1.0)\n\ncombined_mesh = concat_meshes(circle_mesh_A, circle_mesh_B)\n\nplot(combined_mesh)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}","title":"two circles"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/mesh/#Graph-stuff","page":"Mesh","text":"Graphs.jl gives us some nice ways to ask questions about the mesh. How many connected components are there?  (There are two circles, so there should be 2).\n\nusing Graphs\n\nGraphs.count_connected_components(combined_mesh.topology)\n2","title":"Graph stuff"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/mesh/#Rectangles","page":"Mesh","text":"rect = rectangle(\n    SVector(10.0, 10.0),\n    3.0, 4.0, π / 4.0\n)\n\ncircles_and_rect = concat_meshes(combined_mesh, rect)\n\nplot(circles_and_rect)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=8}\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Rectangles"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/example/#**8.**-Example","page":"Example","text":"TODO: Get rid of this later!!  This is copied verbatim from the Literate.jl repository.\n\nThis is an example generated with Literate based on this source file: example.jl. You are seeing the HTML-output which Documenter has generated based on a markdown file generated with Literate.\n\nIt is recommended to have the source file available when reading this, to better understand how the syntax in the source file corresponds to the output you are seeing.","title":"8. Example"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/example/#Basic-syntax","page":"Example","text":"The basic syntax for Literate is simple, lines starting with # is interpreted as markdown, and all the other lines are interpreted as code. Here is some code:\n\nx = 1 // 3\ny = 2 // 5\n2//5\n\nIn markdown sections we can use markdown syntax. For example, we can write text in italic font, text in bold font and use links.\n\nIt is possible to filter out lines depending on the output using the #md, #nb, #jl and #src tags:\n\nThis line starts with #md and is thus only visible in the markdown output.\n\nThe source file is parsed in chunks of markdown and code. Starting a line with #- manually inserts a chunk break. For example, if we want to display the output of the following operations we may insert #- in between. These two code blocks will now end up in different @example-blocks in the markdown output, and two different notebook cells in the notebook output.\n\nx + y\n11//15\n\nx * y\n2//15","title":"Basic syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/example/#Output-capturing","page":"Example","text":"Code chunks are by default placed in Documenter @example blocks in the generated markdown. This means that the output will be captured in a block when Documenter is building the docs. In notebooks the output is captured in output cells, if the execute keyword argument is set to true. Output to stdout/stderr is also captured.\n\nnote: Note\nNote that Documenter currently only displays output to stdout/stderr if there is no other result to show. Since the vector [1, 2, 3, 4] is returned from foo, the printing of \"This string is printed to stdout.\" is hidden.\n\nfunction foo()\n    println(\"This string is printed to stdout.\")\n    return [1, 2, 3, 4]\nend\n\nfoo()\n4-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n 2\n 3\n 4\n\nJust like in the REPL, outputs ending with a semicolon hides the output:\n\n1 + 1;\n\nBoth Documenter's @example block and notebooks can display images. Here is an example where we generate a simple plot using the Plots.jl package\n\nusing Plots\nx = range(0, stop = 6π, length = 1000)\ny1 = sin.(x)\ny2 = cos.(x)\nplot(x, [y1, y2])\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Output capturing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/example/#Custom-processing","page":"Example","text":"It is possible to give Literate custom pre- and post-processing functions. For example, here we insert a placeholder value x = 123 in the source, and use a preprocessing function that replaces it with y = 321 in the rendered output.\n\nx = 123\n123\n\nIn this case the preprocessing function is defined by\n\nfunction pre(s::String)\n    s = replace(s, \"x = 123\" => \"y = 321\")\n    return s\nend\npre (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Custom processing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/example/#documenter-interaction","page":"Example","text":"In the source file it is possible to use Documenter.jl style references, such as @ref and @id. These will be filtered out in the notebook output. For example, here is a link, but it is only visible as a link if you are reading the markdown output. We can also use equations:\n\nint_Omega nabla v cdot nabla u mathrmdOmega = int_Omega v f mathrmdOmega\n\nusing Documenters math syntax. Documenters syntax is automatically changed to \\begin{equation} ... \\end{equation} in the notebook output to display correctly.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Documenter.jl interaction"},{"category":"section","location":"#ElectrostaticOptics","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for ElectrostaticOptics.\n\n","title":"ElectrostaticOptics"},{"category":"method","location":"#ElectrostaticOptics.lagrange_j-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, AbstractVector{T}}} where T<:Real","page":"Home","text":"Definition of the Lagrange basis polynomials We construct it using the Polynomials.jl fromroots function, it's a \"blink and you'll miss it\" implementation. Arguably not super readable (moving parts hidden), but it's literally the definition of the Lagrange basis.\n\nj : the index of the Lagrange basis polynomial to compute (1-based indexing) nodes : the vector of node positions (length = order of the polynomial)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ElectrostaticOptics.lagrange_j"}]
}
